{
  "passage": "The word monsoon comes from the Arabic word mausim, which means season. India's climate is called a monsoon climate because the direction of the winds reverses with the seasons. In summer, the land heats up much faster than the sea. By May, an area of low pressure forms over the plains of north-western India. The air over the Indian Ocean is cooler, so the pressure there is higher. Winds blow from high pressure to low pressure, so moist winds from the ocean begin to move towards the land. These are the south-west monsoon winds. They pick up a great deal of moisture as they cross the warm ocean. The monsoon usually reaches the southern tip of Kerala by the first week of June and spreads over almost the whole country by mid-July. When the moist winds are forced to rise over the Western Ghats, they cool and drop heavy rain on the western slopes. The eastern side receives much less rain because it lies in the rain shadow. Mawsynram in Meghalaya receives one of the highest rainfalls in the world. By October, the land cools and the winds reverse, blowing from the land towards the sea. These are the retreating monsoon winds, which bring rain to the Coromandel coast of Tamil Nadu in October and November.",
